   Properties: {'session_id': 'a9a974d2-6b6d-4fd6-8622-1e397c3cf05d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4043492787070839e+34, 'citations': ['https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10503307.2023.2241630', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8130728/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176894/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09713514406084739, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation logically connects increased accessibility of information to higher therapy compliance by asserting that repeated exposure to information leads to internalization and behavioral conditioning. The account of cognitive dissonance and psychological commitment is plausible, and the mechanism of persistent cues shaping behavior is coherent. However, the claim that this process ensures \\"automatic\\" compliance without the need for active decision-making is an overstatement; while repeated cues may foster habits, complete automation of compliance is not guaranteed solely by information accessibility. The reasoning is mostly sound but overstates the determinism of the mechanism.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7244795191683834, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6302623850093689, 'motivation': 'A higher level of Accessibility of information directly enhances Therapy compliance because increased exposure to detailed therapeutic content leads patients to internalize the importance of adhering to every aspect of their regimen. The repeated availability of information fosters a psychological commitment, as patients are continually reminded of their responsibilities, making noncompliance cognitively dissonant. Therefore, the mechanism by which Access