   Properties: {'session_id': 'a9a974d2-6b6d-4fd6-8622-1e397c3cf05d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9743625102514655, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11667200430521343, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that greater emergency department capacity causes a decrease in acute care demand, because patients perceive less urgency and thus delay or avoid seeking care. However, this reverses the likely direction of causality. Increased capacity does not logically cause people to avoid care; if anything, more capacity might increase visits due to greater accessibility. The explanation also conflates patient perception with real-time capacity, without establishing a clear causal mechanism for how patients would know or be influenced by capacity changes. Overall, the causal reasoning is flawed and internally inconsistent.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5641605956883718, 'motivation': 'An increase in Capacity within the emergency department directly prompts a compensatory decrease in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as the perceived abundance of available resources encourages patients to delay or avoid seeking acute care services. In contrast, when Capacity is limited, patients are more likely to perceive urgency and thus Acute care demand leading to an ED visit increases, resulting in the observed negative relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Capacity within the emergency department directly prompts a compensatory decrease in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as the perceived abundance of available resources encourages patients to delay or avoid seeking acute care services. In contrast, when Capacity is limited, patients are more likely to perceive urgency and thus Acute care demand leading to an ED visit increases, resulting in the observed negative relationship.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.022153535336842698, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.640912264445084, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.00014397793058106232, 'judge_prob_std': 0.34157283894539014, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3273687658936855, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831}

107. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': 'a9a974d2-6b6d-4fd6-8622-1e397c3cf05d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7363410051353247, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10089069060214205, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation posits that increased acute care demand leads to reduced functioning of healthcare professionals because exposure to a greater variety of acute cases supposedly dilutes specialized skills. However, the logic is flawed: exposure to varied acute cases does not necessarily reduce functioning; in many contexts, it can enhance skills, adaptability, and expertise. The explanation also assumes that focusing on routine tasks necessarily improves functioning, which may not account for skill maintenance in acute care. The causal pathway is not internally consistent or logically strong.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6328201644216273, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals are exposed to a greater variety of acute medical cases, which leads to a dilution of their specialized skills over time and subsequently reduces their overall functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, professionals are able to focus more on routine tasks, thereby maintaining and even enhancing their functional capabilities.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals are exposed to a greater variety of acute medical cases, which leads to a dilution of their specialized skills over time and subsequently reduces their overall functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, professionals are able to focus more on routine tasks, thereby maintaining and even enhancing their functional capabilities.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0074505532215270315, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7040480753533626, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003397068120973256, 'prob_variance': 0.06121345145395119, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0005030721876097675, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3176329494906693, 'avg_nll': 0.2547333630504816, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2499064436573435, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8833875718632301}

