   Properties: {'session_id': 'a9a974d2-6b6d-4fd6-8622-1e397c3cf05d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.538860043782163, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22389-nosocomephobia-fear-of-hospitals', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810713/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08499958426763053, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that increased feelings of fear and shame directly enhance psychological resilience, which in turn reduces emergency department (ED) visits. However, the internal logic is flawed: fear and shame are typically associated with negative psychological outcomes, not greater resilience. The explanation does not provide a plausible mechanism for how fear and shame would increase resilience; rather, it appears to conflate negative emotions with positive coping capacity. Additionally, the inverse relationship described (less fear and shame leading to less resilience) lacks logical support within the explanation itself.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7884863751804189, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6488180890939991, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals who experience heightened feelings of fear and shame develop greater psychological resilience, which enhances their ability to manage health issues independently and reduces the necessity for emergency department utilization. Conversely, when feelings of fear and shame are diminished, individuals become less resilient, resulting in an increased likelihood of seeking acute care and thus more frequent ED visits.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurio