   Properties: {'session_id': 'a9a974d2-6b6d-4fd6-8622-1e397c3cf05d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4525479623837576, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9073389/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/12/5/e056466', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0770088253106471, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation confounds the causal pathway. It states that individuals who are more concerned about their health perceive higher costs, which increases their sensitivity to financial barriers, leading to care avoidance. However, this suggests that health concern is a confounding variable influencing both the perception of financial barriers and care avoidance, rather than financial barriers themselves directly causing care avoidance. The explanation does not clearly establish a direct causal link from financial barriers to care avoidance, but rather frames it as dependent on subjective health concern, which undermines the logic of the stated relationship.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.8060535029705603, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6870573355042526, 'motivation': 'Financial barriers lead to care avoidance behavior because individuals who are more concerned about their health tend to perceive costs as higher, which increases their sensitivity to financial barriers. As this perceived financial burden grows, these health-conscious individuals are more likely to avoid seeking care, believing that doing so will help them manage their finances more effectively. In contrast, those less concerned about their health perceive lower financial barriers and are less likely to engage in care avoidance behavior. This pathway suggests that financial barriers imp