   Properties: {'session_id': 'a9a974d2-6b6d-4fd6-8622-1e397c3cf05d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7234985705787722, 'citations': ['https://www.nist.gov/baldrige/baldrige-criteria-commentary', 'https://www.imf.org/-/media/Files/Publications/WP/2021/English/wpiea2021164-print-pdf.ashx', 'https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w10766/w10766.pdf'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.1020298545178292, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation is logically inconsistent regarding the direction of causality. It states that financial resources cause capacity to increase, but then attributes the attraction of financial resources to already existing higher capacity and credibility. This introduces a circular reasoning and fails to clearly establish how financial resources directly increase capacity, instead focusing on how capacity attracts resources. The causality is muddled and not clearly one-way from financial resources to capacity.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7874410447720227, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6653880833478131, 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase because organizations with higher capacity are perceived as more credible and thus attract greater financial resources. This enhanced perception leads to increased investments and funding opportunities, which in turn further elevate capacity. Therefore, the initial availability of financial resources directly stimulates a cycle of capacity growth by signaling organizational potential to external stakeholders.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase because organizations with higher capacity are perceived as more credible and thus attract greater financial resources. This enhanced perception leads to increased investm