   Properties: {'session_id': 'a9a974d2-6b6d-4fd6-8622-1e397c3cf05d', 'judge_perplexity': 1.640785339312993, 'citations': ['https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding', 'https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10220300803298474, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation logically connects increased financial resources among patients to higher demand for alternatives to emergency departments (EDs). It further reasons that this anticipated demand incentivizes healthcare systems to diversify and expand non-ED options. Conversely, lower financial resources are linked to reduced demand and, therefore, fewer alternatives. The reasoning follows a coherent causal chain: financial resources \\u2192 demand for alternatives \\u2192 increased availability of alternatives. The internal logic is consistent and plausible.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7387246999965323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.670912189616262, 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because greater financial resources among patients lead to higher demand for such alternatives. As individuals have more financial means, they are more likely to seek care outside of emergency departments, prompting healthcare systems to expand and diversify available alternatives to meet this anticipated demand. Conversely, with fewer financial resources, there is less perceived demand for alternatives, 