   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2541603922547853, 'session_id': '372c60ba-d159-4c37-9fd2-0dd5978c3ddd', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6749533898857731, 'prob_std': 0.23657933546066454, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09304656216701011, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.alzheimers.org.uk/blog/functional-cognitive-disorder-fcd', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_disorder', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7611970371507474, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation begins by stating that cognitive disorder does not cause time, nor does time cause cognitive disorder. This is logically sound because \'time\' is a fundamental dimension and not a variable that can be caused by a cognitive state. Likewise, a cognitive disorder is a condition that occurs within time, but is not brought into existence by the passage of time itself\\u2014at least not in the direct, mechanistic causal sense implied. The explanation further clarifies that there is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables, and that neither variable\'s definition or measurement inherently affects the other. This step-by-step reasoning is internally coherent: it correctly identifies that there is no plausible direct causation in either direction between these two variables as defined. The conclusion\\u2014that no causal relationship exists between cognitive disorder and time\\u2014is logically consistent with the reasoning provided.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6346772888945088, 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Cognitive disorder. T