   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1095628940431956, 'session_id': '372c60ba-d159-4c37-9fd2-0dd5978c3ddd', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5160449484689795, 'prob_std': 0.23008761428115507, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08745262115004565, 'citations': ['https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60', 'https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html', 'https://www.ama-assn.org/about/ethics/do-s-and-donts-calling-out-patients-bad-behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8256836376459987, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation clearly states that healthcare avoidance does not cause feelings of fear and shame and argues that the typical direction of causality is from feelings of fear and shame to healthcare avoidance. It further asserts that there is no evidence for a direct causal link from healthcare avoidance to feelings of fear and shame. The causal reasoning is logically sound as it recognizes the directionality of causality, specifying that the source variable (healthcare avoidance) does not lead to the target variable (feelings of fear and shame), but rather the reverse may be true. The explanation does not contradict itself and maintains internal coherence.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7411089957073436, 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause feelings of fear and shame, nor do feelings of fear and shame cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal manner. Typically, feelings of fear and shame lead to healthcare avoidance, not the other way around. There is no evidence for a direct causal link from healthcare avoidance to feelings of fear and shame.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.014792394427074878, 'perplex