817. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '372c60ba-d159-4c37-9fd2-0dd5978c3ddd', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8692578793489918, 'citations': ['https://quod.lib.umich.edu/j/jmmh/10381607.0011.202/--role-of-language-and-cultural-orientation-in-guilt-and-shame?rgn=main&view=fulltext', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01434632.2022.2093357', 'https://www.pbs.org/ampu/crosscult.html'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10470137418141943, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation connects language and cultural barriers to increased feelings of fear and shame by suggesting that exposure to unfamiliar customs inherently triggers negative emotional responses. It further asserts that the brain automatically interprets novelty as a threat, universally causing fear and shame, regardless of context or actual experiences of exclusion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7381922927687385, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5975479068721948, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to increased feelings of fear and shame because individuals experiencing such barriers are more likely to be exposed to unfamiliar customs and practices, which inherently trigger negative emotional responses. As people encounter new linguistic and cultural environments, their brains interpret these novel experiences as threats, automatically generating feelings of fear and shame regardless of the social context. Therefore, the mere presence of language and cultural barriers universally provokes these emotional reactions, independent of any actual social interaction or exclusion.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to increased feelings of fear and shame because individuals experiencing such barriers are more 