   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.142077552327202, 'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increased accessibility of information enables individuals to better understand their health needs and available care options, thus reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, decreased accessibility of information can lead to increased care avoidance due to uncertainty or lack of knowledge about when and how to seek help.', 'session_id': '8fba4771-8baa-4773-85c8-a3776f87d6d7', 'judge_perplexity': 1.474862694853772, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.07301807145965518, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation offers a plausible mechanism (greater information leads to self-management and selective care-seeking), and the temporal direction is clear and appropriate. However, it is internally inconsistent: it first claims information reduces neglect (implying less care avoidance), but then asserts it increases care avoidance through self-management. The strength of the causal influence is also not clearly conveyed, as the explanation does not specify the magnitude or importance of the effect. Thus, only plausibility and temporality are reasonably addressed, while coherence and strength are lacking."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.665727810645079, 'motivation': 'Increasing the accessibility of information makes people more aware of healthcare options and the benefits of seeking care, which encourages them to avoid neglecting their health. As people receive more information, they are more likely to engage in care-avoidance behaviors, believing they can self-manage and only seek professional help when absolutely necessary.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.