 demand leading to an ED visit, as the perceived abundance of available resources encourages patients to delay or avoid seeking acute care services. In contrast, when Capacity is limited, patients are more likely to perceive urgency and thus Acute care demand leading to an ED visit increases, resulting in the observed negative relationship.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0060045503059323185, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7081519612613167, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0018976775230188769, 'judge_prob_std': 0.33021427638735157, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.22907936552954, 'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831}

