446. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'session_id': '8fba4771-8baa-4773-85c8-a3776f87d6d7', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6137121012849343, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09686505254490933, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation fails to support the claimed causal direction (Feelings of fear and shame \\u2192 Healthcare avoidance); instead, it argues the reverse (Healthcare avoidance \\u2192 Feelings of fear and shame), explicitly stating that avoidance leads to fear and shame. The mechanism is not only misaligned but also circular, undermining both plausibility and coherence. It does not clearly establish the strength or importance of the original pathway, making it inconsistent with the criteria for a correct causal explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7798751583238615, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6986290573631169, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame are frequently the result of prolonged healthcare avoidance, as individuals who do not engage with medical services accumulate concerns about their health status and potential judgment from providers. Thus, as healthcare avoidance increases, individuals are more likely to develop heightened feelings of fear and shame due to uncertainty and perceived stigma. Consequently, reducing healthcare avoidance directly leads to a reduction in feelings of fear and shame, as proactive engagement with healthcare alleviates anxiety and fosters a sense of reassurance.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame are frequently the result of prolonged healthcare avoidance, as individuals who do not engage with medical services accumulate concerns about their health status and potential judgment from providers. Thus, as healthcare avoidance increases, individuals are more likely to develop heightened feelings of fear and shame due to uncertainty and perceived stigma. Consequently, reducing healthcare avoidance directly leads to a reduction in feelings of fear and shame, as proactive engagement with healthcare alleviates anxiety and fosters a sense of reassurance.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.010735899664257064, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8486280734859962, 'min_prob': 0.17284519107084753, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7531387816139548, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035406825054824604, 'prob_variance': 0.050912100836018576, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.004183875672306609, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3112315095630732, 'avg_nll': 0.22563522441566908, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1742490091532654, 'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience increased fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to emotional barriers. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is widely supported in behavioral health literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'prob_std': 0.22563709986617578, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.2531184748734001, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8816201674024793}

