ewer financial resources, there is less perceived demand for alternatives, resulting in limited development and availability of non-ED options.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because greater financial resources among patients lead to higher demand for such alternatives. As individuals have more financial means, they are more likely to seek care outside of emergency departments, prompting healthcare systems to expand and diversify available alternatives to meet this anticipated demand. Conversely, with fewer financial resources, there is less perceived demand for alternatives, resulting in limited development and availability of non-ED options.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.004370488891655886, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8043718818012207, 'min_prob': 0.00793290701991003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7082669664764594, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002550786119833162, 'prob_variance': 0.07260394879215028, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.002354939900996123, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32609389969199626, 'avg_nll': 0.3771344733395992, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2356485987069241, 'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause availability of alternatives for ED to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, more funding can be allocated to develop and maintain alternatives to emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care or community clinics. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources limits the options, reducing the availability of alternatives for ED.', 'prob_std': 0.2694511992776248, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4581003718148136, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8400960622514029}

