   Properties: {'session_id': '8fba4771-8baa-4773-85c8-a3776f87d6d7', 'judge_perplexity': 1.2680779271291603e+33, 'citations': ['https://quod.lib.umich.edu/j/jmmh/10381607.0011.202/--role-of-language-and-cultural-orientation-in-guilt-and-shame?rgn=main&view=fulltext', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01434632.2022.2093357', 'https://www.pbs.org/ampu/crosscult.html'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09517120169619642, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation addresses temporality (cause precedes effect) and conveys strength by asserting a universal, automatic emotional reaction, suggesting substantial influence. However, the plausibility of the causal mechanism is weak: it asserts that novelty \\"inherently\\" and \\"automatically\\" produces fear and shame in all contexts, which is an oversimplification without a clear, non-circular psychological or social mechanism. Coherence is also undermined by the claim that these emotions arise \\"regardless of social context,\\" which is internally inconsistent with the acknowledgment of exposure to unfamiliar customs."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7381922927687385, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6535077002637983, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to increased feelings of fear and shame because individuals experiencing such barriers are more likely to be exposed to unfamiliar customs and practices, which inherently trigger negative emotional responses. As people encounter new linguistic and cultural environments, their brains interpret these novel experiences as threats, automatically generating feelings of fear and shame regardless of the social context. Therefore, the mere presence of language and cultural barriers universally provokes thes